case-organizer (4.6.2) stable; urgency=medium

  * Admin Settings: administrators can now permanently delete a user
    account, not just deactivate it. A "Delete" button sits beside
    "Deactivate/Activate" in each user row — the two share the row width
    equally — and asks for confirmation before removing the account. The
    delete is guarded so you cannot remove your own account or the last
    active administrator; deleting a user also revokes their sessions and
    (via the schema's foreign keys) removes their messages, password-reset
    tokens and event assignments, while invoices, certificates, notices and
    calendar events they authored are kept with the author cleared. Backed
    by the admin-only Settings "delete_user" action and a new
    services.users.delete_user() helper.
  * Tests: added delete_user service tests (removal, cascade of sessions,
    missing-user no-op) and Settings route guard tests (admin can delete
    another user; self-deletion is refused).

 -- Izumi <swarajswarupaggarwal@gmail.com>  Sat, 11 Jul 2026 14:00:00 +0530

case-organizer (4.6.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * Calendar / event manager: new "Task" event type alongside hearing,
    filing, deadline and appearance, for general to-do items tracked against
    a case. A task carries a due date and pending/done status, can be
    assigned to users, appears in the day agenda's "Due" bucket, is flagged
    overdue when still pending past its date, and is surfaced in the daily
    digest (due-today / overdue / tomorrow). The event-type dropdown, month
    calendar (a "T"-labelled green chip), legend and per-case timeline all
    render the new type.
  * Schema migration v8 -> v9: the case_events.event_type CHECK constraint
    was widened to accept 'task' via a data-preserving table rebuild —
    existing events, ids, self-references and child assignees/participants
    are unchanged.
  * Tests: added task event-type coverage (creation via the API, due-bucket
    placement and overdue behaviour, migration upgrade preserved).

